{#
    The way on from this page: the pages either side of it, at the end of the
    column.

    Nothing of the renderer's is being replaced here — its own prev/next block
    has been commented out of the core template for years, and there is no
    `next` or `prev` to read. So the order comes from `Navigation\Pager`: the
    tree flattened, which is the order the rail lists and the order somebody
    reading a manual front to back would take.

    `.sds-foot` is the shape the class layer already has for it — one row, the
    way out of this page, and all a single page owes its reader. The links are
    buttons because that is what a reader presses at the end of a page, and the
    glyph carries the direction; the label carries the page.

    An accessible name is written for each, direction first: the glyph says
    which way to somebody looking, and a name that is only the page title says
    it to nobody else. It contains the visible label, so the two agree.
#}
{%- set steps = pager() -%}
{%- if steps.previous or steps.next %}
<footer class="sds-foot">
    {%- if steps.previous %}
    <a class="sds-btn sds-btn--secondary" href="{{ steps.previous.href }}" rel="prev"
       aria-label="Previous page: {{ steps.previous.label }}"
        ><sds-icon name="actions-arrow-left" size="16"></sds-icon>{{ steps.previous.label }}</a>
    {%- endif %}
    {%- if steps.next %}
    <a class="sds-btn sds-btn--secondary sds-row__end" href="{{ steps.next.href }}" rel="next"
       aria-label="Next page: {{ steps.next.label }}"
        >{{ steps.next.label }}<sds-icon name="actions-arrow-right" size="16"></sds-icon></a>
    {%- endif %}
</footer>
{%- endif -%}
